  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 407 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nAreas of fog will continue to be found ahead of a clipper system\nthis morning, mainly in the far south central and portions of\nthe southeast. The mentioned clipper system should gradually \nerode this fog away this morning. For now maintained the Dense \nFog Advisory for Emmons, Logan, and McIntosh counties, while \nexpanding it eastward to include LaMoure and Dickey counties, \nthrough the mid morning hours. The mentioned clipper system will\nmove across southern Canada today, with the surface trough \nclipping northern ND. Overall this will be a dry system, which \nwill bring warm, dry, and breezy westerly winds. Forecast highs \ntoday are in the 50s to lower 60s, although abundant high clouds\nmay limit the upper end of this forecast. Decided to blend in\nNBM90th for winds today due to strong 850 mb winds combined \nwith a brief period of pressure rises and cold air advection \nthis afternoon. These could some near advisory level winds this \nafternoon, especially in the north, although confidence isn't \nhigh enough for a wind highlight at this time. These stronger \nwinds combined with lowering humidity values could bring a brief\nperiod of near critical fire weather conditions as well this \nafternoon, mainly in northwestern portions. Winds diminish \ntonight, although dry air in place should limit the fog threat. \nLows tonight are forecast in the mid 20s to mid 30s. A weak \nfront will drape across the east on Sunday bringing slightly \ncooler temperatures in the 50s to these areas. Meanwhile, a \nsteady southerly flow in the west will bring continued warm \ntemperatures in the 60s. Another weak clipper system looks to \nmove across southern Canada for Sunday, although mainly dry \nconditions are again expected. Dry conditions combined with a \nsteady westerly flow will again limit the fog threat for Sunday \nnight. Overnight lows will be mild and near 40 degrees. \n\nZonal to northwest flow will be found to start the week on\nMonday. This continues mild and dry conditions. Breezy westerly\nwinds may also be found as a result. Some lowering of RH on\nMonday could perhaps bring at least some increased fire weather\nconcerns, especially in the southwest. More northwesterly flow\nis then forecast for Tuesday which may cool temperatures\nslightly and diminish the winds. Dry conditions are again\nexpected to persist. Tuesday night into Wednesday, a weak\ndisturbance moves through in the northwest flow which could\nbring some low chances for rain showers. Breezy winds may also\nbe found with this system, especially in the southwest. An\namplified ridge is still forecast to move across the interior US\nlater next week. This will continue dry conditions across the\nforecast area. How far east this ridge moves in will greatly\ndetermine temperatures next week. NBM temperature spreads are \nquite large given the eastward uncertainty. A more eastward\nmovement would bring warmer temperatures, while a stalled ridge\nbrings cooler northwest flow and more normal like temperatures.\nForecast is in the middle currently, yet did lower slightly from\nprevious forecasts.",
